.TH NIGHTCORE 1 "2026-09-25" "nightcore" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nightcore.py \- speed up or pitch-shift audio, losslessly
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nightcore
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIinput\fR]
.br
.B nightcore --pitch-diff
\fIF\fR [\fIF\fR]
.br
.B nightcore --speed-diff
\fIST\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBnightcore.py\fR is a simple wrapper that creates bit-perfect Nightcore by altering the
sample rate of the audio to change speed/pitch file using FFmpeg.
.PP
It can also independently pitch audio with rubberband. Shifting up
first upsamples with SoX (shifted highs would otherwise fold back as
audible garbage); shifting down pitches at the current rate and settles
the rate afterwards (pre-downsampling would brickwall restorable highs).
Either way duration is preserved.
.PP
You can also encode music in a standard sample rate like (ex: 48kHz, 88.2kHz, 96kHz...)
automatically to be chosen to be the smallest standard frequency that's still
higher than the frequency that FFmpeg's `asetrate` changed.
.PP
Most of the flags here should cover 99% of all nightcore tracks from the 2000s.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -s, --speed \fISPEED\fR
Speed factor (1.2 = 20% faster). Default 1.0. Given an \fB.lrc\fR lyrics
file instead of audio, every timestamp (and the \fI[length:]\fR header)
is scaled by 1/\fISPEED\fR; the lyrics mirror the parent audio's output
name (same stem, \fB.lrc\fR extension), falling back to \fIsong
[1.2x].lrc\fR without a same-stem audio file. Only \fB-s\fR and
\fB-r\fR change lyrics; the rest only shape the mirrored name.
.TP
.B -r, --reciprocal
Use 1/speed instead (undo a speedup).
.TP
.B -y, --overwrite
Overwrite existing output files.
.TP
.B -q, --quiet
Drop tool output but keep stage/progress lines. Repeat as
\fB-qq\fR for warnings and errors only; a failed tool's captured
output always prints.
.TP
.B -R, --resample
Resample to next standard rate via SoX. Default keeps the non-standard
rate, bit-perfect.
.TP
.B -B, --both
With
.BR -R ,
also keep the non-standard-rate file.
.TP
.B -F, --format \fIFMT\fR
Output format: \fBflac\fR (default), \fBalac\fR, \fBwav\fR, \fBmp3\fR,
\fBopus\fR, \fBaac\fR (.m4a).
Automatic quality defaults exist only for lossless output, tuned for most
compression (FLAC level 12; ALAC/WAV have no knobs). Lossy outputs prefer
the best encoder -- MP3 via libmp3lame (lame CLI fallback), Opus via
opusenc (ffmpeg libopus fallback), AAC via qaac (ffmpeg AAC fallback with
a quality warning) -- but carry zero baked-in quality: \fB--lossy-opts\fR
is mandatory, and rates are always passed through bit-perfect (\fB-R\fR/
\fB--both\fR are ignored with a warning). If a
weird rate is unacceptable the encoder itself fails loudly. Lossy files
carry only the shift tag in filename and Title: no source-quality or rate
tags. With a genuine \fBqaac\fR on PATH (verified via
.IR "qaac --check" ,
even under wine), ALAC/AAC encode with CoreAudio; otherwise ffmpeg's
encoders are used.
.TP
.B -p, --pitch
Pitch-shift only, keep duration (needs rubberband; SoX upsamples first
so shifted highs survive). Labelled full-precision
semitones, e.g.
.IR "[3.1564128700055254st]" ;
negating the value reverses the shift exactly.
.TP
.B -S, --semitones \fIST\fR
Pitch-shift by semitones: alone it implies \fB-p\fR; with
\fB-s\fR it makes a combined speed+pitch job.
.TP
.B -n, --dry-run
Print output filename(s), convert nothing.
.TP
.B --simple-title \fR[\fITEXT\fR]
Keep the Title tag free of script-added \fI[...]\fR groups (filenames
keep them); optional \fITEXT\fR is appended after a space, e.g.
\fI--simple-title="(Nightcore)"\fR.
.TP
.B --tag-template \fIFILE\fR
Apply a tag template file: one \fIkey="value"\fR entry per tag (values
may span lines). Empty or bare values keep the source tag; tags missing
from the file are stripped from outputs. The computed title suffix is
still appended. Template \fIcomment\fR lands in COMMENT (ffmpeg alone
would file it as DESCRIPTION) and \fIencoder\fR stubs are honored on
FLAC; muxer-written encoder tags are stripped by default.
.TP
.B --cover \fIIMG\fR
PNG/JPG artwork to use as the sole attached picture of outputs (only wav
cannot carry pictures; opus pictures go via opusenc).
.TP
.B --lossy-opts \fIOPTS\fR
REQUIRED for lossy outputs: encoder quality flags, shlex-split, you
choose -- there are no defaults. The run fails loudly without it, naming
an example for the selected encoder (e.g. \fI"-q:a 0"\fR for libmp3lame,
\fI"--bitrate 128"\fR for opusenc, \fI"-V 91"\fR for qaac). The flag
dialect picks the encoder: native-CLI style (\fI-V\fR, \fI--bitrate\fR,
any \fI--long-opt\fR) uses lame/opusenc/qaac, \fI-X:a\fR style uses the
ffmpeg encoder; mixing both is an error. A forced-but-missing encoder
fails loudly instead of falling back. Ignored (with a warning) for
lossless outputs. Values starting with \fB-\fR need the equals form:
\fI--lossy-opts="-q:a 0"\fR.
.TP
.B --batch \fR[\fIDIR\fR]
Convert every audio file in \fIDIR\fR (default: .) instead of one input;
outputs go to \fI./encodes/[format]/\fR (relative to the current working
directory). Tag-derived names can collide there (duplicate files, or
lossy outputs with stripped quality tags): the first file keeps its
name, later ones get \fI" (2)"\fR, \fI" (3)"\fR, ... appended.
Reruns skip existing outputs (add files freely); \fB-y\fR re-encodes
everything from scratch.
\fB.lrc\fR lyrics files mirror their parent audio's output name
(same stem, \fB.lrc\fR extension).
.TP
.B --pitch-diff \fIF\fR [\fIF\fR]
Calculator, no conversion. One value = speed factor; two values = peak
frequencies (original, pitched). Prints semitones, cents, speed factor.
.TP
.B -D, --denightcore
Undo from filename tags: reads [Nx]/[Nst] shift tags (including combined
forms like [1.2x 2.16st]), applies the inverse shift, and removes those
tags. Quality tags stay intact. Fails if no shift tags are found.
.TP
.B --rb-params \fIPARAMS\fR
Extra rubberband flags, appended after the defaults (\fB-3 -p \fIST\fR).
A \fB-p\fR/\fB--pitch\fR inside overrides the pitch amount (warned about)
instead of duplicating it. Recorded extras land in the filename as
\fI[rb ...]\fR so -D inverts with them automatically. Values starting
with \fB-\fR need the equals form: \fI--rb-params="--formant"\fR.
.TP
.B --speed-diff \fIST\fR
Calculator, no conversion: semitones to speed factor.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Speed up 20%:
.PP
.RS
nightcore song.flac -s 1.2 -y
.RE
.PP
Same plus a player-safe resampled copy:
.PP
.RS
nightcore song.flac -s 1.2 -R -B -y
.RE
.PP
Pitch-only remake from measured semitones:
.PP
.RS
nightcore --pitch-diff 54 63
nightcore song.flac -S 2.6687090560373763 -y
.RE
.PP
Sped up and pitched up in one run:
.PP
.RS
nightcore song.flac -s 1.2 -S 2.166866948 -y
.RE
.PP
Undo a 1.2x speedup:
.PP
.RS
nightcore "Song [1.2x].flac" -s 1.2 -r -y
.RE
.PP
Undo from filename tags:
.PP
.RS
nightcore "Song [1.2x].flac" -D -y
.RE
.PP
Retime lyrics for the sped-up audio:
.PP
.RS
nightcore song.lrc -s 1.2 -y
.RE
.PP
Preview names for a bulk rename:
.PP
.RS
for f in *.flac; do nightcore "$f" -s 1.2 -n; done
.RE
.SH AUTHOR
Lev <lev@lusosterone.net>
.br
https://git.lusosterone.net/lev/nightcore
.SH DEPENDENCIES
At bare minimum python3 and FFmpeg. SoX is required for any resampling,
and rubberband for any pitch shifting (-p, -S and pitch -D jobs).
A \fBqaac\fR binary is used for ALAC/AAC when
present, with automatic fallback to FFmpeg (AAC fallback warns: it sounds
worse). \fBlame\fR is the MP3 fallback when ffmpeg lacks libmp3lame;
\fBopusenc\fR is the preferred Opus encoder (ffmpeg libopus fallback). The \fBmetaflac\fR tool
(from the flac package) forces Cover (front) picture types and
exact COMMENT/DESCRIPTION/ENCODER tags on FLAC outputs; a run needing
those fixes refuses to proceed without it (install flac).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR sox (1),
.BR https://aur.archlinux.org/packages/qaac-wine
